Patch 35: give the shipped pre-trellis gate a self-calibrated threshold, so it
can be widened beyond 4K and beyond speed 5.

Against anchor 7368e76. Independent of patch 0034.

WHAT IS ALREADY UPSTREAM. Commit d4ad634 ("Skip trellis based on an estimated
rdcost") added the gate to search_tx_type, scoped to speed >= 5 AND 4K-or-
larger, where it measured:

    A1 (4K), RA, speed 5:   +4.06% speedup / +0.04% BD   ratio 101.5

That is the mechanism working extremely well in the place it was enabled.

WHY IT IS SCOPED SO NARROWLY, AND WHAT THAT COSTS. Earlier CTC runs of the same
gate at Speed 3 gave:

    A1 (4K)    +3.79% / +0.09% BD   ratio 42.1   PASS
    A2 (1080p) +3.03% / +0.15% BD   ratio 20.2   miss (bar 25)

A1 passed comfortably; A2 did not. So the gate is live on one class at one
preset, and off for 1080p at every preset and for speeds 0-4 everywhere.

THE REASON A2 BEHAVES DIFFERENTLY IS STRUCTURAL, NOT INCIDENTAL. The gate
compares an UNTRELLISED estimate of a candidate against best_rd, which is a
TRELLISED figure. The estimate is biased high by however much the trellis would
have lowered it, and the fixed 1/8 margin is a constant pad for that bias. But
the bias is not constant -- it depends on qindex, coefficient density,
transform block size and content. **A bias that differs by 2x between A1 and A2
cannot be paid for by one constant**, and that is exactly what the 42.1 / 20.2
split looks like.

THE CORRECTION IS ALREADY IN HAND. Every candidate that passes the gate has its
pre-trellis estimate computed by the gate, and its true post-trellis RD
computed a few statements below. Accumulating both over the candidates of the
transform block currently being searched gives

    alpha = sum(rd_post) / sum(pre_rd)

the trellis's realised RD reduction measured on exactly the population the gate
is applied to. The gate then compares like with like: alpha * pre_rd against
best_rd.

Because de-biasing makes the estimate smaller, calibration makes the gate SAFER
at a fixed margin, not faster. The point is to spend that safety on a wider
scope and a smaller margin.

THE REFACTOR IS EXACT, AND THAT IS CHECKED, NOT ASSERTED. Built with
-DAVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE=0 and run against the shipped gate (both with the 4K
test relaxed so the gate fires on a testable clip), the two produce
byte-identical bitstreams:

    320x192 cpu-used=5 qp160   63802d27c9d898a5 == 63802d27c9d898a5
    320x192 cpu-used=5 qp110   b7174767a48dd3bf == b7174767a48dd3bf
    640x360 cpu-used=6 qp185   2ef026d2c926744d == 2ef026d2c926744d

So -DAVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE=0 reproduces today's encoder exactly, and every
difference measured against it is attributable to calibration alone.

KNOBS. Defaults preserve the shipped behaviour except for turning calibration
on, so the default build is the smallest possible change from today:

    -DAVM_TX_GATE_MIN_SPEED=N    lowest preset the gate runs at   (default 5)
    -DAVM_TX_GATE_MIN_DIM=N      min(w,h) for the gate to run     (default 2160)
    -DAVM_TX_GATE_MARGIN64=N     residual margin in 64ths          (default 8 = 1/8)
    -DAVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE=0/1  calibration off/on                (default 1)
    -DAVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN=N    samples before alpha is trusted   (default 2)
    -DAVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12  floor on alpha, 2048 == 0.5

DETERMINISM. The accumulators are locals in search_tx_type, scoped to one
transform block's candidate loop, deliberately not carried in MACROBLOCK. The
gate then depends only on the candidates of the block being searched -- never on
tile decomposition, thread count or encode history. Same input, same output, at
any thread count. Overflow is handled by scaling both sums down until the Q12
shift is provably safe rather than by assuming a bound on RD magnitudes; alpha
is clamped to (0.5, 1.0], because above 1.0 the gate would become more
aggressive than its own uncalibrated estimate and below the floor it would
quietly switch itself off.

ARMS TO RUN. The interesting question is whether calibration lets the gate be
widened, so the arms should widen it:

    1  -DAVM_TX_GATE_MIN_DIM=0                      4K gate extended to A2, speed 5
    2  -DAVM_TX_GATE_MIN_DIM=0 -DAVM_TX_GATE_MIN_SPEED=3   ... and down to speed 3
    3  -DAVM_TX_GATE_MIN_DIM=0 -DAVM_TX_GATE_MARGIN64=4    ... with a tighter margin

Arm 1 is the decisive one: A2 at speed 5 with calibration, against A2 at speed 5
without the gate at all (today's encoder). Arm 2 re-asks the Speed-3 question
that produced 42.1 / 20.2, with the bias removed.

LOCAL MEASUREMENT, AND WHY IT IS NOT QUOTED AS A SPEEDUP. Paired wall clock on
the development machine gave -0.5% (margin 8) and +0.9% (margin 4) against the
shipped gate, with reps disagreeing in sign. That is inside the noise floor and
is therefore no measurement at all. It is also the expected shape: at a fixed
margin calibration prunes LESS, so margin 8 should be slightly slower and the
gain has to come from widening the scope -- which local clips cannot test,
because the gate is 4K-only today.

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT. If arm 1 shows A2 still below its bar with calibration
on, then the A1/A2 split is not driven by the estimator's bias, and this whole
approach is wrong; the gate should stay 4K-only and the remaining work is to
find why 1080p responds differently. If arm 1 passes, arm 2 is the prize --
extending a ratio-100 mechanism from one class at one preset to both classes
across several.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
index 4c482ec..a055c7b 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
@@ -2359,6 +2359,12 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
   const bool is_inter = is_inter_block(mbmi, xd->tree_type);
   const bool is_lossless = xd->lossless[mbmi->segment_id];
   int64_t best_rd = INT64_MAX;
+  // Pre-trellis gate self-calibration state. Locals, scoped to this transform
+  // block's candidate loop -- deliberately not carried in MACROBLOCK, so the
+  // gate depends only on the candidates of the block being searched and is
+  // independent of tile decomposition and thread count.
+  int64_t gate_pre_sum = 0, gate_post_sum = 0;
+  int gate_n = 0;
   uint16_t best_eob = 0;
   TX_TYPE best_tx_type = MAKE_TX_TYPE_FROM_PRIMARY_TX_TYPE(DCT_DCT);
   int rate_cost = 0;
@@ -2688,18 +2694,92 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
                                    tx_sf->prune_intra_ist_stx_by_zero_eob)) {
           continue;
         }
-        if (cpi->oxcf.speed >= 5) {
-          const int is_4k_or_larger = AVMMIN(cm->width, cm->height) >= 2160;
-          if (is_4k_or_larger && quant_param.use_optimize_b && !fsc_mode_in &&
-              *eob != 0 && best_rd != INT64_MAX && txw != 64 && txh != 64) {
+        // Pre-trellis RD gate, self-calibrated.
+        //
+        // The shipped gate compares an UNTRELLISED estimate of this candidate
+        // against best_rd, which is a TRELLISED figure. The estimate is biased
+        // high by however much the trellis would have lowered it, and the
+        // fixed 1/8 margin is a constant pad for a bias that is not constant:
+        // it depends on qindex, coefficient density, block size and content.
+        // That is visible in the CTC data -- at Speed 3 the same margin gave
+        // ratio 42.1 on A1 (4K) but only 20.2 on A2 (1080p). A bias that
+        // differs 2x between classes cannot be paid for by one constant, which
+        // is why the shipped gate is restricted to 4K.
+        //
+        // The correction is already in hand. Every candidate that passes the
+        // gate has its pre-trellis estimate computed here and its true
+        // post-trellis RD computed a few statements below. Accumulating both
+        // over this transform block's own candidates gives
+        //
+        //     alpha = sum(rd_post) / sum(pre_rd)
+        //
+        // the trellis's realised RD reduction measured on exactly the
+        // population the gate is applied to, so the gate can compare like with
+        // like: alpha * pre_rd against best_rd.
+        //
+        // De-biasing makes the estimate smaller, so calibration makes the gate
+        // SAFER at a fixed margin rather than faster. The point is to spend
+        // that safety on a smaller margin and on a wider scope.
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_MIN_SPEED
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_MIN_SPEED 5
+#endif
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_MIN_DIM        // 2160 = shipped (4K only); 0 = all sizes
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_MIN_DIM 2160
+#endif
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_MARGIN64       // residual margin in 64ths; 8 == the shipped 1/8
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_MARGIN64 8
+#endif
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE      // 0 reproduces the shipped gate exactly
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE 1
+#endif
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN      // samples before alpha is trusted
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN 2
+#endif
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12  // floor on alpha; 2048 == 0.5
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12 2048
+#endif
+        int64_t this_pre_rd = -1;
+        if (cpi->oxcf.speed >= AVM_TX_GATE_MIN_SPEED) {
+          const int size_ok = AVMMIN(cm->width, cm->height) >= AVM_TX_GATE_MIN_DIM;
+          if (size_ok && quant_param.use_optimize_b && !fsc_mode_in &&
+              *eob != 0 && txw != 64 && txh != 64) {
             const int pre_rate =
                 cost_coeffs(cm, x, plane, block, tx_size, tx_type, CCTX_NONE,
                             txb_ctx, cm->features.reduced_tx_set_used);
             int64_t pre_dist, pre_sse;
             dist_block_tx_domain(x, plane, block, tx_size, &pre_dist, &pre_sse);
             const int64_t pre_rd = RDCOST(x->rdmult, pre_rate, pre_dist);
-            if (pre_rd - (pre_rd >> 3) > best_rd) {
-              continue;
+            this_pre_rd = pre_rd;
+            if (best_rd != INT64_MAX) {
+              // pre_rd is an RD cost for a single transform block and is
+              // bounded well below INT64_MAX/64, so this cannot overflow.
+              int64_t effective =
+                  pre_rd - ((pre_rd * AVM_TX_GATE_MARGIN64) >> 6);
+#if AVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE
+              if (gate_n >= AVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN && gate_pre_sum > 0) {
+                // Scale both sums down until the Q12 shift is provably safe,
+                // rather than assuming a bound on RD magnitudes.
+                int64_t ps = gate_pre_sum, qs = gate_post_sum;
+                while (qs > ((int64_t)1 << 50)) {
+                  qs >>= 1;
+                  ps >>= 1;
+                }
+                if (ps > 0) {
+                  int64_t alpha_q12 = (qs << 12) / ps;
+                  // alpha > 1 would make the gate more aggressive than its own
+                  // uncalibrated estimate, which is never intended; below the
+                  // floor it would quietly switch the gate off.
+                  if (alpha_q12 > 4096) alpha_q12 = 4096;
+                  if (alpha_q12 < AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12)
+                    alpha_q12 = AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12;
+                  effective = (pre_rd * alpha_q12) >> 12;
+                  effective -= (effective * AVM_TX_GATE_MARGIN64) >> 6;
+                }
+              }
+#endif  // AVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE
+              if (effective > best_rd) {
+                continue;
+              }
             }
           }
         }
@@ -2751,6 +2831,18 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
         const int64_t rd =
             RDCOST(x->rdmult, this_rd_stats.rate, this_rd_stats.dist);
 
+#if AVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE
+        // Feed the gate's own estimate and the outcome it was estimating back
+        // into the calibration. Only candidates the gate actually costed
+        // contribute, so alpha is measured over exactly the population the
+        // gate is applied to.
+        if (this_pre_rd > 0 && rd > 0) {
+          gate_pre_sum += this_pre_rd;
+          gate_post_sum += rd;
+          gate_n++;
+        }
+#endif  // AVM_TX_GATE_CALIBRATE
+
         if (is_lossless) {
           assert(this_rd_stats.dist == 0);
         }
